### Description
The Terraform program defined below creates an S3 bucket and configures a lifecycle rule to set a custom Time to Live (TTL) attribute for data expiration. The lifecycle rule specifies that objects in the bucket will expire after a certain number of days, which is defined by the `days` variable. The default value for `days` is set to 365 days.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

variable "bucket_name" {
  description = "The name of the S3 bucket"
  default     = "my-custom-ttl-bucket"
}

variable "days" {
  description = "The number of days after which objects in the bucket will expire"
  default     = 365
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "data_bucket" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = "private"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration" "data_bucket_lifecycle" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.data_bucket.id

  rule {
    id     = "ExpireObjects"
    status = "Enabled"

    expiration {
      days = var.days
    }
  }
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured to use the `us-west-2` region.
2. **Variables**:
   - `bucket_name`: The name of the S3 bucket. The default value is `my-custom-ttl-bucket`.
   - `days`: The number of days after which objects in the bucket will expire. The default value is 365 days.
3. **S3 Bucket Resource**: An S3 bucket named `my-custom-ttl-bucket` is created with private access.
4. **Lifecycle Configuration Resource**: A lifecycle configuration is applied to the S3 bucket. The rule is named `ExpireObjects` and is set to be enabled. The expiration policy specifies that objects will expire after the number of days defined by the `days` variable.

